hv_reset: hypervisor (q.bin) and CPU reset vector (reset.bin)
=============================================================

Build
-----
    make hv_reset                  toolchain is picked from `uname -s`
    make hv_reset TOOLCHAIN=sun    Solaris + Sun Studio, Sun's own makefiles
    make hv_reset TOOLCHAIN=gnu    sparc64-linux-gnu cross tools, no Sun code

Output: $OUTDIR/q.bin and $OUTDIR/reset.bin.

Both paths start the same way: copy src/hypervisor to a work directory,
apply patches/hv to the copy, build in the copy. src/hypervisor is never
modified.

Compile options (-D/-U) are listed in options.mk; the reasons are in
README.options.


Sun toolchain (TOOLCHAIN=sun)
-----------------------------
This is Sun's own build: Makefile.master, Makefile.platform, SysV make and
Sun Studio cc, used as they are. Three tool macros are overridden on the make
command line -- AS, LD and OBJCOPY -- and all three point at system tools.
No prebuilt tool from the OpenSPARC release is used.

The assembler. The hypervisor runs in hyperprivileged mode and uses
registers normal code never sees: %hpstate, %htstate, %htba, %hver, %hintp,
%hstick_cmpr, read and written with rdhpr/wrhpr. Sun's assembler enables
their names with one option:

    -xarch=v9d          (greatlakes/Makefile.family:62, ASFLAGS)

v9d is not a public arch level; the assembler's usage text lists only
v7 ... v9b. Which assemblers accept it:

  - /usr/ccs/bin/as from the s10u11 install media (118683-07): rejects it.
    Every %h* register is an "invalid register".
  - Sun Studio 11 fbe: rejects it.
  - /usr/ccs/bin/as with patch 118683-15 (Studio 12.5 Compiler Common):
    accepts it. This is what the sun path uses; AS_SUN in config.mk.
    check-sun-toolchain tries a real rdhpr before the build starts.
    REQUIREMENTS.SOLARIS says where the patch comes from.

The linker. The patched assembler adds a .SUNW_cap section (hardware
capabilities, a side effect of -xarch=v9d) to every object. ld puts that
section in its own PT_LOAD after .bss and moves _etext there, 12 MB past the
image. start_master uses _etext as the end of the ROM-to-RAM copy, so the
copy would be 12 MB instead of 250 KB. sun/nocap.mapfile zeroes the
capability mask and ld then emits no such section; hv_reset.mk passes it as
a second -M on every link.

Two more tools are needed. Both are built from src/hypervisor/src/support:

  stabs   Generates offsets.h. The assembler code reads C struct fields by
          constant offsets (STRAND_*, CPU_*, ...), which Sun computes at
          build time: a stub file is compiled with -xdebugformat=stabs -g -S,
          and stabs reads offset, size and stride from the debug records.

              cc -xdebugformat=stabs -g -S -o offsets-n1.s offsets-n1.c
              stabs -t genassym -m lp64 offsets.tmp < offsets-n1.s > offsets.h

          It is used as a struct-layout tool, not for debugging.

  aschk   Checks assembler source. Makefile.platform has
          `q: chkasm $(OBJS)`, so aschk runs before q.bin is linked. It
          cannot be skipped.

Nothing prebuilt is needed, and replacing it changes nothing. Checked by
building the unmodified hypervisor from the OpenSPARC T2 source release
both ways:
  - source-built stabs/aschk against Sun's prebuilt ones: offsets.h and
    reset.bin byte-identical; q.bin differs only in the build timestamp and
    directory path;
  - system as + nocap.mapfile against Sun's prebuilt assembler: reset.bin
    identical; q.bin differs only in the timestamp.
No difference in code or data either time. (Those comparisons predate the
build stamp: the timestamp is now the commit's, so two builds of one commit
give an identical q.bin. The build directory, which Sun cc records in a debug
stub of each C object, no longer lands in q.bin: mcs removes the stubs before
the link, patches/hv/hv-sun-strip-debug.patch -- see the comment in
build/hv_reset.mk.)


GNU toolchain (TOOLCHAIN=gnu)
-----------------------------
A port, not a rebuild. gnu/build.sh replaces Sun's makefiles and toolchain.
It does five things:

  - Rewrites syntax that Sun's as accepts and GNU as rejects: gnu/fixup.sed
    and the fix() calls in build.sh, each with a comment.
  - Reimplements `.pushlocals` / `.poplocals` (gnu/pushlocals.py).
  - Links with its own linker script, q.ld, instead of Sun's mapfile.
  - Adds a memcpy shim. gcc emits memcpy calls for struct copies, Sun's cc
    did not, and the hypervisor has no memcpy of its own.
  - Runs Sun's aschk, as `q: chkasm` does on the sun path: every load and
    store in the .s files that names an offsets.h constant is checked against
    offsets.chk, and a mismatch stops the build. aschk runs no SPARC code; it
    is built from Sun's source with two fixes for a Linux host
    (patches/hv/gnu/aschk-host-build.patch) by the host compiler (HOST_GCC,
    HOST_CSTD). tests/full/hv_reset/internal/aschk.sh shows it fails on a
    wrong size.

offsets.h cannot be made the Sun way (gcc removed -gstabs in GCC 13, and Sun's
stabs reads only Sun's dialect anyway), but it can be made with Sun's
semantics: dwarf2genassym.pl compiles a stub that refers to every type
offsets.in names with -gdwarf-4, reads the layout from
`readelf --debug-dump=info`, and prints what `stabs -t genassym` prints. The
result is byte-identical to the offsets.h Sun's stabs makes for this target,
gnu/reference/offsets.ontario-legion.h. The same script reads Developer Studio
12.6's DWARF (dwarfdump) on the Sun path.

The output is not byte-identical to the sun build and is not meant to be:
the assemblers encode the same constants differently (alignment padding, how
synthetic instructions such as setx are expanded), the linker scripts differ,
and the C code comes from another compiler. Both boot to the same OBP banner.
Details: gnu/README.
